  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "The future tense in Hindi is formed using familiar endings 'गा', 'गे', and 'गी' for masculine singular, masculine plural, and feminine subjects respectively. There are also internal vowel changes: 'ऊँ' with the subject 'मैं', 'ए' with all other singular subjects, and 'एँ' with all other plural subjects. Verbs like 'लिखना' (write) and 'पाना' (find) demonstrate typical future conjugations: 'लिखूँगा/लिखूँगी' (I will write), 'लिखेगा/लिखेगी' (he/she will write), 'लिखेंगे/लिखेंगी' (they will write). Similarly, 'पाऊँगा/पाऊँगी', 'पाएगा/पाएगी', 'पाएँगे/पाएँगी' show the pattern for 'पाना'.\nSome verbs have contracted future forms: 'देना' (to give) becomes 'दूँगा/दूँगी' in the first person singular and 'देगा/देगी' for other singular subjects; 'लेना' (to take) becomes 'लूँगा/लूँगी' and 'लेगा/लेगी'; 'होना' (to be) becomes 'हूँगा/हूँगी' or regular forms like 'होऊँगा, होएगा' etc.\nThe verb 'देना' combined with an infinitive in the oblique case expresses giving permission or allowing someone to do something. For example, 'आने दीजिये' means 'Please let me come', 'मैं आपको बोलने देता हूँ' means 'I give you permission to speak', 'वे हमें लिखने देंगे' means 'They will let us write', and 'हम बच्चों को स्कूल जाने देते थे' means 'We used to let the children go to school'.\n"
    }
